column15:
Let $X$ and $Y$ be two discrete random variables with alphabets $\mathcal{X}$ and $\mathcal{Y}$ and a joint probability distribution $p(x,y)$, then \  egin{equation} I(X,Y) \geq 0 \end{equation}  egin{proof} By definition \  egin{equation} I(X,Y) = \sum_{x \in \mathcal{X}} \sum_{y \in \mathcal{Y}} p(x,y) \, log  rac {p(x,y)}{p(x)p(y)} \end{equation} \vspace{0.3cm} 
oindent Since f(z) = log(z) is a concave function, we can apply Jensen's inequality changing the inequality direction \  egin{equation} - I(X,Y) \leq log \sum_{x \in \mathcal{X}} \sum_{y \in \mathcal{Y}} p(x,y) \,  rac {p(x)p(y)}{p(x,y)} \end{equation} \vspace{0.3cm}  egin{equation} - I(X,Y) \leq log \sum_{x \in \mathcal{X}} \sum_{y \in \mathcal{Y}} p(x)p(y) \end{equation} \vspace{0.3cm} 
oindent From the normalization axiom of probability theory, it follows \  egin{equation} \sum_{x \in \mathcal{X}} \sum_{y \in \mathcal{Y}} p(x)p(y) = 1 \end{equation} \vspace{0.3cm} 
oindent Hence \  egin{equation} - I(X,Y) \leq log 1 = 0 \iff I(X,Y) \geq 0 \end{equation} \end{proof}

